course: biology
lesson: 107
title: The Asian forest area: giant pandas, red pandas, tapirs and sun bears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The giant panda sits like a person on a sofa, holds a bamboo stem in its paw and munches for up to 14 hours a day, because bamboo gives little energy and its gut, built like a meat-eater's, digests only a small part.
explain: To grip the stems the panda uses a «thumb» that is not a thumb at all but a long wrist bone grown out, a famous example of evolution reusing an old part for a new job.
explain: Close by, a red panda sleeps in a tree wrapped in its bushy tail, a tapir wades into a pond with its short trunk, and a binturong leaves a smell of buttered popcorn along its branch.
explain: In Kung Fu Panda, Po gobbles dumplings and his teacher Shifu is a red panda; real giant pandas live on bamboo, and the red panda is not even a close relative, though both evolved a wrist «thumb» separately.
fact: Giant pandas eat bamboo for up to 14 hours a day, often 12 to 38 kilograms, and digest only a small part, because they are bears whose teeth and gut come from meat-eating ancestors.
fact: A panda's «thumb» is a long wrist bone that helps it grip bamboo, and the «panda's thumb» is a famous example of evolution reusing an old part.
fact: Almost all giant pandas in zoos abroad are lent by China, and cubs born there go to China; Edinburgh Zoo had the only giant pandas in Britain from 2011 to 2023.
fact: After China protected bamboo forests, made reserves and stopped hunting, wild giant pandas grew to over 1800, and in 2016 they moved from «endangered» down to «vulnerable».
fact: Bamboo flowers and dies all at once every few decades, so pandas need several kinds of bamboo nearby.
fact: Red pandas are not close relatives of giant pandas but a family of their own; both eat bamboo and both have a wrist «thumb», evolved separately, and the red panda was called «panda» first.
fact: Tapirs are shy forest animals with short, bendy snouts, related to horses and rhinos; they escape danger by running into water, and their babies have stripes and spots like a watermelon that hide them in the forest light.
fact: The binturong, or bearcat, is an Asian tree animal whose urine smells like buttered popcorn because it holds the same chemical, and it has a gripping tail.
fact: Sumatran tigers are the smallest tigers, live only on the island of Sumatra and number fewer than about 600, because forests are cut for farms and tigers are hunted; zoos keep a breeding group as insurance.
fact: Asian small-clawed otters live in big families and find crabs and shellfish by feeling under stones with clever, finger-like paws; otters for cafés and the pet trade are often caught illegally from the wild, which harms wild otters.
fact: Asian forests have great hornbills, peacocks and golden pheasants; peahens choose males with the best tails of eye spots, and a peacock grows a new tail every year.
fact: Sun bears are the smallest bears and use very long tongues and long claws to reach honey and insects in logs; sun bears often stand up on their back legs, and in 2023 people claimed a Chinese zoo's sun bear was a person in costume, but it was a real bear.
exercise: How long can a giant panda spend eating bamboo in a day? | Up to about 14 hours | About 10 minutes | Only one hour at dawn | It eats only once a week
exercise: Why must a panda eat so much bamboo? | Bamboo gives little energy and its gut digests only a little | Bamboo is its only drink as well | It stores bamboo in its fat cheeks to eat later in the day, like a hamster | Pandas are always growing taller
exercise: What is a panda's «thumb» really? | A long wrist bone that helps it grip | A real thumb like a person's | An extra finger it grows each year | A hard pad of skin on its palm
exercise: Who owns almost all giant pandas in zoos abroad? | China, which lends them | The zoo that bought them | Whoever looks after them longest | Nobody, they are wild animals
exercise: Why were giant pandas moved from «endangered» to «vulnerable» in 2016? | Their numbers grew after forests were protected | All the wild pandas were moved into zoos to be safe | They learned to eat new food | Scientists stopped counting them
exercise: Why do pandas need several kinds of bamboo nearby? | Each kind flowers and dies all at once every few decades | Pandas soon get bored of eating only one taste of bamboo every day | Each kind grows only one leaf a year | Bamboo hides them from tigers
exercise: Is the red panda a small kind of giant panda? | No, it is a family of its own | Yes, it is a baby giant panda | Yes, a giant panda with red fur | No, it is a kind of fox
exercise: Which animal was called «panda» first? | The red panda | The giant panda | The raccoon | The sun bear
exercise: Which animals are tapirs related to? | Horses and rhinos | Elephants and mammoths | Pigs and hippos | Anteaters and sloths
exercise: Why do baby tapirs have stripes and spots? | They hide them in the dappled forest light | They warn hungry animals that the babies taste bad | They help the mother count them | They fade away as soon as the baby swims
exercise: What does a binturong's urine smell like? | Buttered popcorn | Fresh flowers | Burnt toast | Peppermint
exercise: About how many Sumatran tigers are left in the wild? | Fewer than about 600 | About 60,000 tigers | Only two | About a million
exercise: Why are Sumatran tigers in danger? | Forests are cut for farms and tigers are hunted | They have mostly been eaten by other, bigger tigers | They cannot find water on Sumatra | Their stripes are too easy to see
exercise: How do Asian small-clawed otters find crabs? | By feeling under stones with finger-like paws | By listening for them under the stones with their long, pointed ears | By diving with their eyes wide open | By smelling them from the riverbank
exercise: Why does a peacock show off its big tail? | Peahens pick males with the best tails | It scares hungry tigers away | It keeps the peacock cool | It helps it fly fast
exercise: What does a sun bear use its very long tongue for? | Licking honey and insects from logs | Catching fish in rivers | Cleaning its cubs' eyes and ears | Cooling down on hot days
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Po loves dumplings. What do real giant pandas eat? | Almost only bamboo | Mostly dumplings and noodles | Mostly fish from rivers | Mostly fruit from trees
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Shifu the red panda trains Po the giant panda. Are they closely related? | No, they are separate families that both evolved a wrist «thumb» | Yes, the red panda is a young giant panda | Yes, they are the same species | No, Shifu is really a raccoon
test: «pandas are bears that went vegetarian, innit» What is right? | Yes, they are bears whose teeth and gut still come from meat-eaters | No, pandas are really a big kind of raccoon from the mountains of China | No, pandas have always eaten only plants | No, pandas eat mostly meat in the wild
test: Each front paw of a panda has 5 fingers plus the wrist «thumb». How many gripping parts on both front paws? | 12 | 10 | 6 | 14
test: 2 tapirs, 1 elephant and 3 red pandas. How many trunks or snouts like trunks? | 3 | 6 | 1 | 2
test: What would happen to pandas if one kind of bamboo flowered and died in their forest? | They would move to other kinds, if there are some nearby | They would start to eat tigers | They would sleep all through the years until it grows back | Nothing, pandas do not eat bamboo
test: «zoo's sun bear is a bloke in a costume, look at it standing!» What is true? | It is a real bear; sun bears can stand up like that | Yes, zoos sometimes use costumes when a real bear is ill | Yes, all sun bears are fakes | No, it is a dog in a bear suit
test: «where's the popcorn smell coming from??» at the Asian area. What is the answer? | A binturong marking its paths | The café next door is making popcorn | A peacock fluffing its feathers | The red panda eating corn
test: «wanna go to an otter café and cuddle one» Why is that a bad idea? | Otter cafés feed an illegal trade that harms wild otters | Otters hate people and always bite anyone who touches them | Otters cannot live in buildings at all | It is fine, all those otters are bred to be pets
test: Why does the zoo keep a breeding group of Sumatran tigers? | As insurance if wild ones die out | To sell their stripes | To make them bigger than other tigers | Because they are too dangerous in the wild
test: A baby tapir looks nothing like its plain grey mum. Why? | Its stripes and spots hide it until it grows | It is a different animal that she found and adopted | It has rolled in muddy leaves | It will become a zebra
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of the giant panda? | It consists almost wholly of bamboo, of which only a small part is digested by a gut adapted to meat | It consists principally of meat, supplemented by bamboo | It consists of fish and fruit in equal measure | It consists of bamboo, which the panda digests completely
exam: Identify the nature of the so-called thumb of the giant panda. | An elongated bone of the wrist which assists in grasping stems of bamboo | A true opposable thumb identical in every respect to that of man and the ape | An additional finger acquired anew each year | A hardened pad of skin without any bone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the change in the conservation status of the giant panda in 2016. | It was reclassified from endangered to vulnerable following the protection of its forests | It was declared extinct in the wild | It was reclassified from vulnerable to critically endangered following further heavy losses | It was removed from all lists, its numbers being beyond counting
exam: State the relationship between the red panda and the giant panda. | They are not closely related, each having evolved a wrist thumb independently | The red panda is the juvenile form of the giant panda | They are a single species differing only in colour | The giant panda is descended directly from the red panda within the last million years
